  "transcript": "# Tourism\n\n## How holiday habits and household finances have shifted\n> People are taking shorter, cheaper trips, moving to self-catering and STRs, and this is squeezing hotels and attractions.\n\nChair: To start: have tourists' expectations and priorities changed in recent years?\nChris Haslam: Yes. UK holidays are shorter — average now about 9.6 days overall and short breaks down from 3.3 to 2.9 days — and people are spending less since the 2021 post‑pandemic peak.\nChris Haslam: There's a big shift toward short-term rentals (STRs), which now take around 20–25% of the market, with hotels down to about 40–45%.\nVicky Philpott: Travellers want new experiences they can share on social media — not just lying on a beach. Families look for value and activities that keep kids entertained.\nChair: How are UK destinations responding to tighter household budgets?\nChris Haslam: Not well. Many places haven't adapted business models for shorter stays: spas, 24-hour dining or staff costs stay the same while spending per visitor falls.\nVicky Philpott: Families increasingly choose self-catering to save money; online 'travel hacks' and budget tips are widespread to stretch holiday budgets.\n\n\n## Reviving coastal towns and managing seasonality\n> Some towns succeed by attracting funding or engaging communities; extending the season is essential to sustainability.\n\nMr Bayo Alaba: What can seaside towns like Southend do to attract more overnight visitors?\nChris Haslam: Two models work: external funding and grants (Great Yarmouth) or community-led efforts (Bude). Both can revive a town if sustained and well managed.\nChris Haslam: But the British seaside faces long-term decline: overnight stays down 18% in 10 years and many resorts are maintained for short summer seasons only.\nVicky Philpott: Events help — music, food and local festivals bring day visitors who then return for longer stays; making places 'Instagrammable' also helps draw new crowds.\nNigel Huddleston: Seasonality is the big issue. Extending shoulder months would help jobs and productivity — how do we do it with unpredictable UK weather?\nChris Haslam: Extend the season with new products — 'coolcations' for people escaping hotter summers abroad, wetsuits for kids, autumn festivals — and better marketing.\n\n\n## Experience, cleanliness and environmental confidence\n> Visitors care about cleanliness, public facilities and safe bathing water — sewage discharges and closed toilets put people off.\n\nNigel Huddleston: What practical things make a place stand out?\nVicky Philpott: Cleanliness, helpful staff, activities for children, clear information or an app — those add value beyond the headline price.\nChris Haslam: Simple basics matter: clean toilets, emptied bins, fair car parking and functioning services — failing these angers visitors more than fancy attractions.\nVicky Philpott: A major barrier is bathing-water quality. Sewage discharges (CSOs) and reports of pollution put people off going to the sea.\nChris Haslam: DEFRA posts seasonal testing results, but those are historic; CSO discharges happen unpredictably. Many rely on apps like Surfers Against Sewage to check real-time safety.\n\n\n## Digital discovery, influencers, AI and weather information\n> Social media drives discovery more than official promotion, AI will change planning, and better weather icons could stop lost visitor income.\n\nJeff Smith: What most influences where people go now?\nVicky Philpott: Viral TikToks and Instagram posts; social content often trumps guides. Influencers are used but organic posts sometimes have bigger reach.\nChris Haslam: Traditional travel journalism still matters for critical evaluation, but social media is hugely influential. Our paper still gets strong UK destination interest.\nJeff Smith: Have you seen AI in travel?\nChris Haslam: Yes — AI agents can copy itineraries from a video and personalise them using users' online data. Agents see it as a tool, not a total replacement for humans.\nJeff Smith: Jamie, you mentioned weather apps affecting attendance. Explain that.\nJamie Christon: Visitors check a single weather icon and may cancel if it shows rain. Research suggests that misinterpreted icons can cost attractions like Chester Zoo large sums in a single day.\nJamie Christon: We want icons that show four points in the day (morning, lunchtime, afternoon, evening) so people make better decisions about visiting.\n\n\n## Workforce pressures, regulation and what Government could change\n> Rising costs, low pay progression, transport gaps, regulation gaps for mixed organisations like zoos, and under-resourced national promotion need concrete Government action.\n\nMr Bayo Alaba: What stops recruiting and keeping staff?\nJamie Christon: Pay and progression: chefs are hard to recruit, and many leave for steadier roles. Zoos offer specialist careers but training is zoo-funded with little Government support.\nAlex Polizzi: Staff housing, poor rural public transport and small allowances for staff accommodation make it hard. Many small businesses can't afford to house staff locally.\nAlex Polizzi: Rising costs hit small hotels hard: Polizzi group saw nearly £1m added to costs from business rates, utilities, national insurance and wage rises — owners often don't pay themselves.\nDr Rupa Huq: What about short-term lets and tax fairness?\nAlex Polizzi: Airbnbs undercut some businesses and benefit from different tax/relief rules; small B&Bs face heavy health and safety and letting rules but less favourable council tax treatment for second homes.\nJamie Christon: Zoos fall between Departments: conservation and animal welfare sit with DEFRA, but we are also major employers, educators and regional economic drivers — we miss funding that heritage bodies access.\nNigel Huddleston: Would a dedicated tourism Minister help?\nAlex Polizzi: Yes — someone who understands and represents hospitality's issues. Also, create a professional centre of excellence to make hospitality an aspirational career.\nJamie Christon: For zoos, simplify Government contact by putting relevant responsibilities together so we don't fall through departmental cracks.\nChair: If each of you could ask the Government for one change to help UK tourism in five years, what would it be?\nJamie Christon: Give sectors like zoos a clear, single departmental home so policy and funding fit what we actually do — conservation, education, tourism and scientific research in one place.\nAlex Polizzi: Professionalise hospitality: invest in recognised training and a centre of excellence so the industry attracts and keeps skilled people and parents support careers in hospitality.\nChair: Any final points from the first panel?\nVicky Philpott: Affordability in school holidays and sewage pollution are the two big barriers for domestic coastal tourism.\nChris Haslam: Bathing-water quality is a reputational crisis: we risk being seen again as the 'sick man of Europe' if sewage issues aren't fixed.\nChair: Thank you all — your evidence highlights concrete actions: fix coastal water quality, improve weather information, back season-extension, resolve regulatory gaps and invest in workforce training.\n",
